<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sopoćani Monastery</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1260s</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sopoćani is a Serbian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> monastery near Novi Pazar in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, founded by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-uros-i">Stefan Uroš I</a> in the 1260s as his burial church. Its frescoes, painted around 1265, are among the finest works of 13th-century Byzantine-style painting. It forms part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> Stari <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a> and Sopoćani.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The monastery stands near the source of the Raška River in the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a>, the centre of the medieval Serbian state, 15 kilometres west of Novi Pazar. It was an endowment of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-uros-i">Stefan Uroš I</a>, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-the-first-crowned">Stefan the First-Crowned</a> and a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-nemanja">Stefan Nemanja</a>, and was meant as his burial place. The church is dedicated to the Holy Trinity. The painting of its main parts can be dated indirectly to between 1263 and 1270. Stefan Uroš I was buried there, as were Anna Dandolo and Joanikije I. The church was extended and renovated in the mid-14th century by his great-grandson Dušan, when it took on the character of a cathedral.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Of a larger complex that once held dining rooms and residential buildings inside a high stone wall with two gates, only the Church of the Holy Trinity remains. The decoration follows a plan formed over the 13th century. The chancel has liturgical scenes, the nave shows Christ's work of salvation through the cycle of the Great Feasts, and the narthex has <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-testament">Old Testament</a>, dogmatic and eschatological themes. Portraits of the Nemanjić family and scenes from the lives of Simeon Nemanja and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-sava">Saint Sava</a> appear as well, and Archbishop Sava II, head of the Serbian Orthodox Church from 1263, is shown in the procession of archbishops near the altar.</p><p>The style draws on late Byzantine art and shows a transition from the Komnenian to the Palaiologan period. The fresco of the Dormition of the Virgin on the western wall of the nave, with stately compositions and monumental figures, is the best known. Some experts on Serbian medieval art consider the frescoes the most beautiful of their time. The origins of the painters are unknown. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-uros-i">Stefan Uroš I</a> ordered gold-sheet tesserae for the outlines of some of the more prominent frescoes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The monks had to leave the monastery temporarily several times in the 16th century because of the Ottoman threat. In 1689 the Ottoman Turks set fire to it and carried off the lead from the church roof. The brotherhood fled with relics to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> and did not return, and the site stayed deserted for over two hundred years. The vaults caved in and the dome fell. Large restoration campaigns took place in 1925-1929 and 1949-1958, and the church has been settled again by monks.</p><p>Copying of Sopoćani's work in 1370-1375 created a Sopoćani style that spread to other churches, and in the early 20th century its art inspired modern Serbian artists. It was declared a Monument of Culture of Exceptional Importance in 1979 and was added that year to the World Heritage list together with Stari Ras.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sopoćani” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
